[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me also verify the regular sequence I found and present the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHilbert series argument as an alternative/cleaner CM check.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hilbert series approach: Since I is homogeneous (all generators degree 2), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mR/I is graded. The Hilbert series is H(R/I, t) = (1 - 6t² + 8t³ - 3t⁴)/(1-t)⁶. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mWe showed (1-t)³ | (1 - 6t² + 8t³ - 3t⁴) and the quotient is 1 + 3t. So H(R/I, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mt) = (1+3t)/(1-t)³. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a graded quotient of a polynomial ring, if the Hilbert series can be written[0m[38;2;124;124;124mas h(t)/(1-t)^d where h(1) > 0 and h has non-negative coefficients, then... [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwell, the dimension is d = 3 (the order of pole at t=1). And CM-ness: R/I is CM [0m[38;2;124;124;124miff the h-vector (coefficients of h) is... actually, the Hilbert series being of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe form (polynomial with non-negative coefficients)/(1-t)^d is necessary but [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnot sufficient for CM in general. But if we know the resolution has length d = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdim, that's sufficient.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the cleanest self-contained proof combines:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Dimension 3: from the Hilbert series, the pole order at t=1 is 3.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- CM: from the minimal free resolution having length 3 = dim, via Auslander-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBuchsbaum.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOr even simpler: the Hilbert series (1+3t)/(1-t)³ with h-vector (1,3) — for a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdomain that's a quotient of a polynomial ring, having the numerator be a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpolynomial (not a power series) with the denominator being (1-t)^dim already [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtells us the resolution is finite of length = dim... no, that's not quite right [0m[38;2;124;124;124meither.[0m

